[2026-06-06 11:47:45] [INFO] [PostToolBatch] Input: {"session_id":"f19f2dcd-5cb7-4e25-89c6-4a90c9705548","transcript_path":"C:\\Users\\Administrator\\.claude\\projects\\D--CodeDevelopment-CodeProject-claude-hk\\f19f2dcd-5cb7-4e25-89c6-4a90c9705548.jsonl","cwd":"D:\\CodeDevelopment\\CodeProject\\claude-hk","permission_mode":"auto","agent_id":"ac3cae781e8ba5d63","agent_type":"Explore","hook_event_name":"PostToolBatch","tool_calls":[{"tool_name":"Read","tool_input":{"file_path":"D:\\CodeDevelopment\\CodeProject\\claude-hk\\CLAUDE.md"},"tool_use_id":"call_5f62a87fe5ff4f5f9f7c91b4","tool_response":"1\t# CLAUDE.md\n2\t\n3\tThis file provides guidance to Claude Code (claude.ai/code) when working with code in this repository.\n4\t\n5\t## Project Overview\n6\t\n7\tTwo coupled trees that act as one product:\n8\t\n9\t- **`.claude/`** — shell-side automation: 29 lifecycle hooks, 14 skills, shared logging/locking/backend-calling modules. Lives inside the repo and is consumed by the Claude Code CLI at runtime.\n10\t- **`claude_tap_plus/`** — Go-side service: a reverse proxy that records Claude API traffic as JSONL traces, plus a backend HTTP server (SQLite-backed) that the shell side calls for atomic issue claims, session registration, and status tracking.\n11\t\n12\tThe two trees communicate only over HTTP on `127.0.0.1`, with `~/.claude-tap-plus/backend.json` as the single config bridge. See [`.claude/` ↔ `claude_tap_plus/` Linkage](#claude--claude_tap_plus-linkage) for the full call map.\n13\t\n14\t## Architecture\n15\t\n16\t### Hooks Pipeline\n17\t\n18\t**Two-layer: `base.sh` (dispatcher) + sibling scripts (business logic)**\n19\t\n20\t`settings.json` registers all 29 Claude Code lifecycle events (`SessionStart` through `SessionEnd`), each pointing to `hooks/XX-event-name/base.sh`. Each `base.sh` sources the shared `hooks/base.sh` (stdin JSON, `json_get()` parsing, structured logging, `hook_output()`), then calls sibling `.sh` scripts.\n21\t\n22\t**Hooks with dispatch logic:**\n23\t- `01-session-start` — first run triggers `init.sh`; every session checks UTF-8/Python/dirs/skill health\n24\t- `03-user-prompt-submit` — calls `skill-inject.sh` to detect `/skill-name` and inject context\n25\t- `05-pre-tool-use` — two-layer interception: `enforce_boundary.sh` (tool whitelist) + `dispatch_to_skill` (skill-level)\n26\t- `16-stop` — runs active skill's `16Stop.sh` for cleanup; `skill-register.sh` auto-registers new skills\n27\t- `29-session-end` — dispatches to active skill, then calls backend `/api/issue/release-session` to auto-release claimed issues\n28\t- `02`, `04`, `06`-`15`, `17`-`28` — logging only (forward to active skill via `dispatch_to_skill`)\n29\t\n30\t**Shared infrastructure** (`hooks/base.sh`):\n31\t- `json_get()` — jq first, fallback `json_get.py` via Python, final sed fallback\n32\t- `dispatch_to_skill(event_num)` — reads `.active` for current session's skill, calls `skills/<skill>/scripts/<EventName>.sh`\n33\t- `hook_output(exit_code, json)` — wraps exit code and result JSON\n34\t\n35\t**Platform** (`hooks/platform.sh`): `uname -s` → `OS_TYPE`; Python prefers embedded `.claude/localLanguage/python/python.exe`.\n36\t\n37\t### Skill Lifecycle\n38\t\n39\tSkills are functional units triggered by `/skill-name`. Each has a full lifecycle:\n40\t\n41\t```\n42\tUser types /skill-name\n43\t  → 03 skill-inject.sh matches registry.conf → runs 03UserPromptSubmit.sh (context injection)\n44\t    → writes .active (session_id|skill_name)\n45\t      → events 05-29 forwarded to skill scripts via dispatch_to_skill()\n46\t        → event 16 runs 16Stop.sh → removes .active entry\n47\t```\n48\t\n49\t**Skill directory structure and script convention:**\n50\t\n51\t```\n52\tXXX-skill-name/\n53\t├── SKILL.md                           # Definition (frontmatter: name/description/allowed-tools)\n54\t└── scripts/\n55\t    ├── 03UserPromptSubmit.sh          # Context injection (runs when /skill-name triggered)\n56\t    ├── 16Stop.sh                      # Cleanup after response (removes .active entry)\n57\t    ├── init.sh                        # Project first-time init (optional)\n58\t    └── init_check.sh                  # Per-session environment check (optional)\n59\t```\n60\t\n61\tScript names follow hook event numbers: `02Setup.sh`, `05PreToolUse.sh`, `08PostToolUse.sh`, etc. `dispatch_to_skill()` in `hooks/base.sh` maps numbers to script names.\n62\t\n63\t**Adding a new skill:**\n64\t1. Create `skills/XXX-name/` with `SKILL.md`, `scripts/03UserPromptSubmit.sh`, `scripts/16Stop.sh`\n65\t2. `skill-register.sh` (runs on `16-stop` event) auto-scans and registers into `registry.conf`; manual add also works\n66\t3. Every `.sh` script must set `SKILL_TAG=\"xxx-name\"` before sourcing `log.sh`\n67\t\n68\t**Key shared modules:**\n69\t- `skills/active.sh` — `.active` file CRUD (`active_add/get/remove`), uses `lock.sh` for file locking\n70\t- `skills/backend.sh` — shared backend API call helpers (`_backend_available`, `_call_backend`, `_get_session_id`, `update_issue_status`); sourced by 003 skill scripts\n71\t- `skills/log.sh` — dual-write logging: unified `hooks/logs/<date>.log` + module `skills/log/<tag>/<date>.log`\n72\t- `skills/lock.sh` — cross-platform file lock via `mkdir` atomic operation (`lock_acquire/release`)\n73\t- `skills/enforce_boundary.sh` — parses `SKILL.md` frontmatter `allowed-tools`, blocks unauthorized tool calls in `05-pre-tool-use`\n74\t\n75\t### 003 Issue Workflow\n76\t\n77\tThe 003 series is a complete issue-driven development pipeline:\n78\t\n79\t```\n80\t/001-1-issue-init → /001-2-issue → /001-3-issue-discuss\n81\t                                       |\n82\t/001-9-issue-review <- /001-8-issue-test <- /001-7-issue-pr <- /001-6-issue-done <- /001-5-issue-fix <- /001-4-issue-claim\n83\t```\n84\t\n85\t- `/001-1-issue-init` — initialize labels (one-time), reads `labels.conf`\n86\t- `/001-4-issue-claim` — atomic claim via backend API (`POST /api/issue/claim`), falls back to label-based claim if backend unavailable\n87\t- `/001-5-issue-fix` — generates branch name from issue labels (bug→fix, enhancement→feat), creates branch\n88\t- `/001-7-issue-pr` — creates PR; **must include `## Test plan` section** (each item as `- [ ]` checkbox)\n89\t- `/001-8-issue-test` — executes Test Plan items, checks off `- [x]`\n90\t- `/001-9-issue-review merge/reject` — **blocks merge if any Test Plan item is unchecked `[ ]`**\n91\t\n92\t### `.claude/` ↔ `claude_tap_plus/` Linkage\n93\t\n94\tThe shell side (`.claude/`) and the Go side (`claude_tap_plus/`) are wired together through **one config file + two twin wrapper modules + a fixed set of HTTP endpoints**. Each side has a stable directory role; changes to one side's path or API shape must be mirrored on the other.\n95\t\n96\t#### Config bridge (single source of truth)\n97\t\n98\t```\n99\t~/.claude-tap-plus/backend.json   {\"host\":\"127.0.0.1\",\"port\":8080}\n100\t        ↑                                   ↓\n101\t        │ written on startup                │ read on every hook/skill fire\n102\t        │ deleted on exit                   │\n103\tclaude_tap_plus/internal/backend/      .claude/lib/config.sh\n104\t                                      (load_backend_config() → sets $BACKEND_URL)\n105\t```\n106\t\n107\t`backend.json` is the contract. The Go backend writes it on `backend` subcommand start and removes it on exit; shell code never parses the file directly, it always goes through `.claude/lib/config.sh`.\n108\t\n109\t#### Twin wrapper modules (intentional duplication)\n110\t\n111\tTo keep `hooks/` and `skills/` trees independent (skills must not source hooks, and vice versa), the same backend-calling primitives are provided twice — both source the same `lib/config.sh`:\n112\t\n113\t| Wrapper | Sourced by | Provides |\n114\t|---------|-----------|----------|\n115\t| `.claude/hooks/lib/backend.sh` | `hooks/01-session-start`, `hooks/29-session-end` | `_backend_available`, `_require_backend`, `_call_backend` |\n116\t| `.claude/skills/backend.sh`   | all `001-*` skill scripts | same, plus `_get_session_id`, `update_issue_status`, `_status_to_label`, `_sync_github_label` |\n117\t\n118\tIf you add a new endpoint, expose it through the wrapper that matches your caller's tree. Don't reach across.\n119\t\n120\t#### Directory-to-directory call map\n121\t\n122\t| Shell caller (`.claude/`) | HTTP endpoint | Go handler (`claude_tap_plus/internal/backend/`) | Purpose |\n123\t|--------------------------|---------------|--------------------------------------------------|---------|\n124\t| `hooks/01-session-start/base.sh` | `POST /api/proxy/trace-init` | `api/` → `service/` | Notify backend a new Claude session is starting |\n125\t| `hooks/01-session-start/base.sh` | `POST /api/session/register` | `api/session_handler.go` → `service/session_service.go` | Register session ↔ machine ↔ project |\n126\t| `hooks/29-session-end/base.sh`   | `POST /api/issue/release-session` | `api/issue_handler.go` → `service/issue_service.go` → `store/issue_store.go` | Release every issue the ending session had claimed |\n127\t| `hooks/29-session-end/base.sh`   | `POST /api/session/close` | `api/session_handler.go` | Mark session closed in DB |\n128\t| `skills/001-4-issue-claim/scripts/03UserPromptSubmit.sh` | `POST /api/issue/check` | `api/issue_handler.go` | Batch check whether issues are already claimed |\n129\t| `skills/001-4-issue-claim/scripts/03UserPromptSubmit.sh` | `POST /api/issue/claim`  | `service/issue_service.go` (atomic) | Atomically claim an issue; falls back to GitHub labels if backend unreachable |\n130\t| `skills/001-5/6/7/8/9-issue-*` (via `update_issue_status`) | `POST /api/issue/status` | `service/issue_service.go` | Push status transitions (`claimed`→`fixing`→…→`merged`); backend returns `previous_status` so the shell can sync GitHub labels |\n131\t\n132\t#### End-to-end issue flow (which tree does what, when)\n133\t\n134\t```\n135\t.claude/skills/001-4-issue-claim/      claude_tap_plus/internal/backend/\n136\t  POST /api/issue/check  ────────────►   service/issue_service.go\n137\t  POST /api/issue/claim ────────────►   (atomic SQL update on issue_claims)\n138\t        │                                       │\n139\t        │ claim granted                          │\n140\t        ▼                                       ▼\n141\t.claude/skills/001-5..001-9/            (status stored in SQLite)\n142\t  update_issue_status(status) ────────►  POST /api/issue/status\n143\t        │                                       │\n144\t        │ previous_status returned              │\n145\t        ▼                                       ▼\n146\t  _sync_github_label()                  (no further backend work)\n147\t  gh issue edit --add-label X\n148\t        │\n149\t        ▼\n150\t  normal GitHub flow (branch / PR / Test Plan / review)\n151\t\n152\t— session ends —\n153\t\n154\t.claude/hooks/29-session-end/base.sh\n155\t  POST /api/issue/release-session ────► release every claim held by this session\n156\t  POST /api/session/close          ────► mark session closed\n157\t```\n158\t\n159\t#### Failure mode\n160\t\n161\tAll shell → backend calls are **silent-fail**:\n162\t- `_backend_available()` → returns nonzero; caller skips the API work and continues.\n163\t- `_require_backend()` (claim only) → returns 0/1/2 so claim can fall back to label-based locking if backend is missing or down.\n164\t\n165\tThis means the shell side keeps working when the Go backend isn't running — the Go side is an enhancement (atomicity, multi-agent coordination, audit trail), not a dependency.\n166\t\n167\t### Initialization Flow\n168\t\n169\tFirst session: `01-session-start/base.sh` detects missing `.initialized` → runs `init.sh`:\n170\t1. Reads `dirs.conf` to create directories\n171\t2. Detects platform + installs Python (downloads embeddable version on Windows)\n172\t3. Configures UTF-8 in `~/.bashrc` (with idempotent marker guards)\n173\t4. Runs each skill's `init.sh`\n174\t5. Writes `.initialized` marker\n175\t\n176\tEvery session: re-checks UTF-8, Python, directory integrity, and each skill's `init_check.sh`.\n177\t\n178\t### Skills Summary\n179\t\n180\t| Skill | Output Directory | Purpose |\n181\t|-------|-----------------|---------|\n182\t| `002-2-doc-testcode-python` | `doc/testcode/python/{api,other}/` | Python test scripts and utilities |\n183\t| `002-1-doc-otherdoc` | `doc/otherDoc/YYYY-MM-DD/` | General documentation by date |\n184\t| `001-1-issue-init` | — | Initialize issue label system (one-time) |\n185\t| `001-2-issue` | `doc/issues/{drafts,templates}/` | Create GitHub Issue |\n186\t| `001-3-issue-discuss` | — | Pull issue content for discussion |\n187\t| `001-4-issue-claim` | — | Atomically claim issue (backend API or label fallback) |\n188\t| `001-5-issue-fix` | — | Create branch from issue and start development |\n189\t| `001-6-issue-done` | — | Mark development complete, ready for PR |\n190\t| `001-7-issue-pr` | — | Create PR linked to issue |\n191\t| `001-8-issue-test` | — | Execute PR's Test Plan |\n192\t| `001-9-issue-review` | — | Review PR: merge or reject |\n193\t| `999-2-git-push` | — | Commit (grouped, Chinese messages) + push |\n194\t| `999-1-git-commit` | — | Commit only (grouped, Chinese messages), no push |\n195\t| `999-other-110-requirement-planning` | `requirement/prds/` | Requirement planning: generate PRD page docs and domain module docs |\n196\t\n197\t## claude_tap_plus (Go Project)\n198\t\n199\tA Go rewrite of claude-tap — a reverse proxy that intercepts Claude Code API traffic, records JSONL traces, and prints token usage summaries. Also includes a backend service for issue claim management.\n200\t\n201\t**Build & Run:**\n202\t```bash\n203\tcd claude_tap_plus\n204\tgo build -o claude-tap-plus ./cmd/claude-tap\n205\tgo run ./cmd/claude-tap claude          # Proxy mode (default)\n206\tgo run ./cmd/claude-tap backend         # Backend service mode\n207\tgo run ./cmd/claude-tap session-push    # Collect sessions\n208\t```\n209\t\n210\t**Subcommands:**\n211\t- `(default)` — proxy mode: intercept API traffic, record traces\n212\t- `backend [--port 8080] [--db backend.db]` — start backend HTTP server for issue/session management\n213\t- `session-push/pull/status` — session sync between `~/.claude/` and local storage\n214\t\n215\t**Architecture:**\n216\t\n217\tProxy path:\n218\t- `cmd/claude-tap/main.go` — CLI entry: flag parsing, proxy lifecycle, child process management, subcommand routing\n219\t- `internal/config/` — Client config, upstream URL detection, `~/.claude.json` reader\n220\t- `internal/proxy/` — Reverse proxy: request forwarding, header redaction, path whitelist, SSE/non-SSE handling\n221\t- `internal/sse/` — SSE byte stream reassembler → complete API response reconstruction\n222\t- `internal/trace/` — JSONL trace writer with token statistics (Anthropic-specific fields)\n223\t- `internal/usage/` — Multi-provider token field normalization (Anthropic/OpenAI/Gemini)\n224\t- `internal/session/` — Session push/pull/status: collect `~/.claude/` sessions to local storage and restore\n225\t\n226\tBackend path:\n227\t- `cmd/claude-tap/backend_cmd.go` — `backend` subcommand entry: parse flags, create and start server\n228\t- `internal/backend/server.go` — HTTP server setup: wire handlers → services → stores\n229\t- `internal/backend/config.go` — Backend config (host, port, DB path), defaults to `127.0.0.1:8080`\n230\t- `internal/backend/api/` — HTTP handlers: `router.go` (routes), `issue_handler.go` (check/claim/release), `session_handler.go`, `health_handler.go`, request/response types\n231\t- `internal/backend/domain/` — Entity structs: `issue.go`, `machine.go`, `project.go`, `session.go`\n232\t- `internal/backend/service/` — Business logic: `issue_service.go`, `session_service.go`, `cleanup_service.go`\n233\t- `internal/backend/store/` — SQLite persistence layer:\n234\t  - `sqlite.go` — `SQLiteStore` with WAL mode, auto-migration on open\n235\t  - `migrations.go` — Schema: 4 tables (`machines`, `projects`, `sessions`, `issue_claims`)\n236\t  - `issue_store.go` — `IssueStore` interface + SQLite impl (check/claim/release/release-session)\n237\t  - `store.go` — Interfaces: `IssueStore`, `Store`\n238\t\n239\t**Backend API routes** (shell-side callers are mapped to these in the [Linkage](#claude--claude_tap_plus-linkage) section above):\n240\t- `GET /health`\n241\t- `POST /api/proxy/trace-init`, `POST /api/session/register`, `POST /api/session/close`\n242\t- `POST /api/issue/{check, claim, release, release-session, status}`\n243\t\n244\t**Data flow (proxy):** CLI parses flags → detects upstream URL → starts local proxy → intercepts requests → SSE reassembler for streaming → JSONL trace per API call → exit summary (API calls, token counts).\n245\t\n246\t**Testing:**\n247\t```bash\n248\tcd claude_tap_plus\n249\tgo test ./...                                       # All tests\n250\tgo test ./internal/sse/...                          # Specific package\n251\tgo test ./tests/backend/...                         # Backend API tests\n252\tgo test ./tests/integration/...                     # Integration tests\n253\tgo test ./tests/backend/issue_api_test.go -run TestClaim  # Single test\n254\t```\n255\t\n256\t**Test structure:**\n257\t- `tests/backend/` — Backend API tests (issue API, session API, concurrency)\n258\t- `tests/integration/` — End-to-end skill flow tests (backend + skill script interaction)\n259\t- `tests/e2e/` — Proxy + trace end-to-end tests\n260\t- `tests/proxy/`, `tests/session/`, `tests/sse/`, `tests/trace/`, `tests/usage/` — Unit tests per package\n261\t\n262\t**Dependencies:** Pure Go SQLite driver (`modernc.org/sqlite`), no CGO required.\n263\t\n264\t## Git Commit Convention (Skills 004/005)\n265\t\n266\tAll commits use Chinese: `<type>: <主描述>` with `- 具体修改描述` sub-items.\n267\t\n268\tTypes: fix/feat/update/style/refactor/perf/test/docs/revert/build/chore\n269\t\n270\tGrouping priority: type → directory/module → functional association → impact scope. Never `git add .` or `git add -A` — always add specific files by group.\n271\t\n272\t\n273\t\n274\t# CLAUDE.md\n275\t\n276\tBehavioral guidelines to reduce common LLM coding mistakes. Merge with project-specific instructions as needed.\n277\t\n278\t**Tradeoff:** These guidelines bias toward caution over speed. For trivial tasks, use judgment.\n279\t\n280\t## 1. Think Before Coding\n281\t\n282\t**Don't assume. Don't hide confusion. Surface tradeoffs.**\n283\t\n284\tBefore implementing:\n285\t- State your assumptions explicitly. If uncertain, ask.\n286\t- If multiple interpretations exist, present them - don't pick silently.\n287\t- If a simpler approach exists, say so. Push back when warranted.\n288\t- If something is unclear, stop. Name what's confusing. Ask.\n289\t\n290\t## 2. Simplicity First\n291\t\n292\t**Minimum code that solves the problem. Nothing speculative.**\n293\t\n294\t- No features beyond what was asked.\n295\t- No abstractions for single-use code.\n296\t- No \"flexibility\" or \"configurability\" that wasn't requested.\n297\t- No error handling for impossible scenarios.\n298\t- If you write 200 lines and it could be 50, rewrite it.\n299\t\n300\tAsk yourself: \"Would a senior engineer say this is overcomplicated?\" If yes, simplify.\n301\t\n302\t## 3. Surgical Changes\n303\t\n304\t**Touch only what you must. Clean up only your own mess.**\n305\t\n306\tWhen editing existing code:\n307\t- Don't \"improve\" adjacent code, comments, or formatting.\n308\t- Don't refactor things that aren't broken.\n309\t- Match existing style, even if you'd do it differently.\n310\t- If you notice unrelated dead code, mention it - don't delete it.\n311\t\n312\tWhen your changes create orphans:\n313\t- Remove imports/variables/functions that YOUR changes made unused.\n314\t- Don't remove pre-existing dead code unless asked.\n315\t\n316\tThe test: Every changed line should trace directly to the user's request.\n317\t\n318\t## 4. Goal-Driven Execution\n319\t\n320\t**Define success criteria. Loop until verified.**\n321\t\n322\tTransform tasks into verifiable goals:\n323\t- \"Add validation\" → \"Write tests for invalid inputs, then make them pass\"\n324\t- \"Fix the bug\" → \"Write a test that reproduces it, then make it pass\"\n325\t- \"Refactor X\" → \"Ensure tests pass before and after\"\n326\t\n327\tFor multi-step tasks, state a brief plan:\n328\t```\n329\t1. [Step] → verify: [check]\n330\t2. [Step] → verify: [check]\n331\t3. [Step] → verify: [check]\n332\t```\n333\t\n334\tStrong success criteria let you loop independently. Weak criteria (\"make it work\") require constant clarification.\n335\t\n336\t---\n337\t\n338\t**These guidelines are working if:** fewer unnecessary changes in diffs, fewer rewrites due to overcomplication, and clarifying questions come before implementation rather than after mistakes."}]}
